{"title": "Twenty (film)", "pageid": 45653377, "revid": 1366923125, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Twenty_%28film%29", "lang": "en", "year": 2015, "short_description": "2015 South Korean film", "wikidata_id": "Q17564587", "is_disambiguation": false, "infobox": {"Hangul": "스물", "RR": "Seumul", "MR": "Sŭmul", "Directed by": "Lee Byeong-heon", "Written by": "Lee Byeong-heon", "Produced by": "Jeong Hoon-tak", "Starring": ["Kim Woo-bin", "Lee Jun-ho", "Kang Ha-neul"], "Cinematography": "Noh Seung-bo", "Edited by": "Nam Na-yeong", "Music by": "Kim Tae-seong", "Distributed by": "Next Entertainment World", "Release date": "March 25, 2015 (2015-03-25)", "Running time": "115 minutes", "Country": "South Korea", "Language": "Korean", "Box office": "US$22.3 million"}, "facts": {"directors": ["Lee Byeong-heon"], "writers": ["Lee Byeong-heon"], "producers": ["Jeong Hoon-tak"], "cast": ["Kim Woo-bin", "Lee Jun-ho", "Kang Ha-neul"], "music": ["Kim Tae-seong"], "cinematography": ["Noh Seung-bo"], "editors": ["Nam Na-yeong"], "production_companies": [], "distributors": ["Next Entertainment World"], "release_dates": ["March 25, 2015 (2015-03-25)"], "runtime_minutes": 115, "countries": ["South Korea"], "languages": ["Korean"], "budget": [], "box_office": ["US$22.3 million"], "based_on": []}, "text": {"lead": "Twenty (Korean: 스물; RR: Seumul) is a 2015 South Korean coming-of-age comedy-drama film starring Kim Woo-bin, Lee Jun-ho, and Kang Ha-neul. It was written and directed by Lee Byeong-heon, his second feature after the 2012 indie Cheer Up, Mr. Lee.", "plot": "Chi-ho, Kyung-jae and Dong-woo are best friends who've just graduated from high school and turned 20. Chi-ho is an unemployed struggling to find a job in the meantime and whose number one priority is dating and chasing women. Kyung-jae is a goody-two-shoes university student whose goal is to get accepted at a corporate job; he's extremely shy around girls, but completely transforms when he gets drunk. And happy-go-lucky Dong-woo dreams of becoming a Manhwa artist, but when his family goes bankrupt, he's forced to become the breadwinner and take on several part-time jobs.", "cast": "- Kim Woo-bin as Chi-ho\n- Lee Jun-ho as Dong-woo\n- Kang Ha-neul as Kyung-jae\n- Jung So-min as So-min\n- Lee Yu-bi as So-hee\n- Min Hyo-rin as Jin-joo\n- Jung Joo-yeon as Eun-hye\n- Kim Eui-sung as Chi-ho's father\n- Yang Hyun-min as So-joong\n- Choi Chamsarang as Sa-rang\n- Ahn Jae-hong as In-gook\n- Na In-woo as Dong-won\n- Baek Soo-hee as Min-jung\n- Song Ye-dam / Song Ye-joon as Twins\n- Han Joon-woo as Club DJ\n- Heo Joon-seok as Beom-soo\n- Jung Yeo-jin as Beom-soo's wife\n- Kim Jong-soo as Dong-woo's uncle\n- Cha Min-ji as 20 year old woman\n- Hong Wan-pyo as Assistant director\n- Oh Ha-nee as Make-up team\n- Kim Chan-hyeong as Head\n- So Hee-jung as Kyung-jae's mother\n- Park Myung-shin as Chi-ho's mother\n- Park Hyuk-kwon as Film director\n- Oh Hyun-kyung as Dong-woo's mother\n- Kim Jae-man as Chicken house boss\n- Choi Il-gu as News anchor", "production": "Two singles were released as the film's \"special\" soundtrack. Part 1 (released on March 6, 2015) featured a duet by Lee Junho and Lee Yu-bi titled \"Cupid's Arrow,\" and Part 2 (released on March 18, 2015) featured \"Twenty\" sung by boyband Sweet Sorrow with narration by Kim Woo-bin.", "release": "South Korea:\nTwenty was released in South Korea on March 25, 2015. It topped the box office in its opening weekend, drawing 1,136,866 viewers and earning ₩8.74 billion (US$7.93 million). As of April 19, 2015, it has grossed US$21.3 million with nearly 3 million admissions.\nInternational:\nThe film was released by CJ Entertainment America in 25 theaters across North America on April 17, 2015.\nIt received a theatrical release in other Asian countries such as Japan, Taiwan, Hong Kong, Singapore, Malaysia, and Vietnam.", "reception": "", "themes": ""}, "sections": {"Lead": "Twenty (Korean: 스물; RR: Seumul) is a 2015 South Korean coming-of-age comedy-drama film starring Kim Woo-bin, Lee Jun-ho, and Kang Ha-neul. It was written and directed by Lee Byeong-heon, his second feature after the 2012 indie Cheer Up, Mr. Lee.", "Plot": "Chi-ho, Kyung-jae and Dong-woo are best friends who've just graduated from high school and turned 20. Chi-ho is an unemployed struggling to find a job in the meantime and whose number one priority is dating and chasing women. Kyung-jae is a goody-two-shoes university student whose goal is to get accepted at a corporate job; he's extremely shy around girls, but completely transforms when he gets drunk. And happy-go-lucky Dong-woo dreams of becoming a Manhwa artist, but when his family goes bankrupt, he's forced to become the breadwinner and take on several part-time jobs.", "Cast": "- Kim Woo-bin as Chi-ho\n- Lee Jun-ho as Dong-woo\n- Kang Ha-neul as Kyung-jae\n- Jung So-min as So-min\n- Lee Yu-bi as So-hee\n- Min Hyo-rin as Jin-joo\n- Jung Joo-yeon as Eun-hye\n- Kim Eui-sung as Chi-ho's father\n- Yang Hyun-min as So-joong\n- Choi Chamsarang as Sa-rang\n- Ahn Jae-hong as In-gook\n- Na In-woo as Dong-won\n- Baek Soo-hee as Min-jung\n- Song Ye-dam / Song Ye-joon as Twins\n- Han Joon-woo as Club DJ\n- Heo Joon-seok as Beom-soo\n- Jung Yeo-jin as Beom-soo's wife\n- Kim Jong-soo as Dong-woo's uncle\n- Cha Min-ji as 20 year old woman\n- Hong Wan-pyo as Assistant director\n- Oh Ha-nee as Make-up team\n- Kim Chan-hyeong as Head\n- So Hee-jung as Kyung-jae's mother\n- Park Myung-shin as Chi-ho's mother\n- Park Hyuk-kwon as Film director\n- Oh Hyun-kyung as Dong-woo's mother\n- Kim Jae-man as Chicken house boss\n- Choi Il-gu as News anchor", "Music": "Two singles were released as the film's \"special\" soundtrack. Part 1 (released on March 6, 2015) featured a duet by Lee Junho and Lee Yu-bi titled \"Cupid's Arrow,\" and Part 2 (released on March 18, 2015) featured \"Twenty\" sung by boyband Sweet Sorrow with narration by Kim Woo-bin.", "Release": "South Korea:\nTwenty was released in South Korea on March 25, 2015. It topped the box office in its opening weekend, drawing 1,136,866 viewers and earning ₩8.74 billion (US$7.93 million). As of April 19, 2015, it has grossed US$21.3 million with nearly 3 million admissions.\nInternational:\nThe film was released by CJ Entertainment America in 25 theaters across North America on April 17, 2015.\nIt received a theatrical release in other Asian countries such as Japan, Taiwan, Hong Kong, Singapore, Malaysia, and Vietnam."}, "cast_list": [{"actor": "Kim Woo-bin", "character": "Chi-ho", "note": ""}, {"actor": "Lee Jun-ho", "character": "Dong-woo", "note": ""}, {"actor": "Kang Ha-neul", "character": "Kyung-jae", "note": ""}, {"actor": "Jung So-min", "character": "So-min", "note": ""}, {"actor": "Lee Yu-bi", "character": "So-hee", "note": ""}, {"actor": "Min Hyo-rin", "character": "Jin-joo", "note": ""}, {"actor": "Jung Joo-yeon", "character": "Eun-hye", "note": ""}, {"actor": "Kim Eui-sung", "character": "Chi-ho's father", "note": ""}, {"actor": "Yang Hyun-min", "character": "So-joong", "note": ""}, {"actor": "Choi Chamsarang", "character": "Sa-rang", "note": ""}, {"actor": "Ahn Jae-hong", "character": "In-gook", "note": ""}, {"actor": "Na In-woo", "character": "Dong-won", "note": ""}, {"actor": "Baek Soo-hee", "character": "Min-jung", "note": ""}, {"actor": "Song Ye-dam / Song Ye-joon", "character": "Twins", "note": ""}, {"actor": "Han Joon-woo", "character": "Club DJ", "note": ""}, {"actor": "Heo Joon-seok", "character": "Beom-soo", "note": ""}, {"actor": "Jung Yeo-jin", "character": "Beom-soo's wife", "note": ""}, {"actor": "Kim Jong-soo", "character": "Dong-woo's uncle", "note": ""}, {"actor": "Cha Min-ji", "character": "20 year old woman", "note": ""}, {"actor": "Hong Wan-pyo", "character": "Assistant director", "note": ""}, {"actor": "Oh Ha-nee", "character": "Make-up team", "note": ""}, {"actor": "Kim Chan-hyeong", "character": "Head", "note": ""}, {"actor": "So Hee-jung", "character": "Kyung-jae's mother", "note"